agraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Anyway, my absolute favorite is a story where a doctor talks to the wife of her colleague. The wife knows that her husband’s cheating with her on someone at the hospital but doesn’t suspect the doctor. You can figure out who’s doing the cheating with the husband, what with the doctor’s own failing marriage, but it’s never explicitly stated. You get the real choice at the end of the story, that it was the protagonist’s choice not to say what’s going on.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Either way, I really enjoyed the vast majority of these stories. The author has a really good knack for getting and holding my attention.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'Both Ways Is the Only Way I Want It - Maile Meloy', '', 'inherit', 'closed', 'closed', '', '187-revision-v1', '', '', '2019-12-20 02:18:29', '2019-12-20 02:18:29', '', 187, 'https://benyakiredits.com/uncategorized/187-revision-v1/', 0, 'revision', '', 0),
(198, 2, '2019-12-20 02:18:39', '2019-12-20 02:18:39', '<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>I read this self-proclaimed novella in a small break before my next hold is ready at the library, and I was very happy with my choice!</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It’s a Robin Hood fable but with frogs. That’s about all you need to know, though I don’t mind going into more detail. The story is great and a nice little distraction. The puns are lots of fun too. If I had my druthers, I’d make more of them, but I’m not the writer, and the writer took great care with this story. Though the novella is written tongue-in-cheek, it’s family friendly and reminded me of the funny, plucky Robin Hood stories I’d been raised with.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It’s a good, earnest work and deserving of respect. There’s a lot of flim-flam in writing, and this has none of it. The author wanted to tell a story, and he told one, one that I quite enjoyed.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'Ribbit Hood - Edward Van Winkle', '', 'inherit', 'closed', 'closed', '', '192-revision-v1', '', '', '2019-12-20 02:18:39', '2019-12-20 02:18:39', '', 192, 'https://benyakiredits.com/uncategorized/192-revision-v1/', 0, 'revision', '', 0),
(199, 2, '2018-11-05 02:20:00', '2018-11-05 02:20:00', '<!-- wp:paragraph {\"fontSize\":\"large\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-large-font-size\">Rating: 4.5 stars</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The short: A quick, enjoyable (and somewhat superficial) book rooted in the post-war Jewish culture in Toledo</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The long: I don’t have much to say, to be honest. I had a good time reading the book, practically a relaxing experience during this week (all of them seem to be difficult in their own way). The book is about the titular Solomon who falls in love with Mollia, a woman who recently lost her husband. The book revolves around the development of their romance but also a few hijinks. The book affirms a positive message with no eye turned to anything except its plot and the Jewish community.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>As an aside, I was raised in an Orthodox Jewish community (but haven’t participated in it since I was 13), and one nice thing about the book is that it felt authentic to me. I don’t have much experience with the Conservative community so I can\'t swear to its authenticity, but this book conveyed the hospitality and friendliness of the Jewish community.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'Solomon the Accountant - Edward M. Knauss', '', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', 'solomon-the-accountant', '', '', '2021-03-13 04:57:27', '2021-03-13 04:57:27', '', 0, 'https://benyakiredits.com/?p=199', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(200, 2, '2019-12-20 02:20:49', '2019-12-20 02:20:49', '', 'Solomon the Accountant - Edward M. Knauss', '', 'inherit', 'closed', 'closed', '', '199-revision-v1', '', '', '2019-12-20 02:20:49', '2019-12-20 02:20:49', '', 199, 'https://benyakiredits.com/uncategorized/199-revision-v1/', 0, 'revision', '', 0),
(201, 2, '2019-12-20 02:22:33', '2019-12-20 02:22:33', '<!-- wp:paragraph {\"fontSize\":\"large\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-large-font-size\">Rating: 4.5 stars</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The short: A quick, enjoyable (and somewhat superficial) book rooted in the post-war Jewish culture in Toledo</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The long: I don’t have much to say, to be honest. I had a good time reading the book, practically a relaxing experience during this week (all of them seem to be difficult in their own way). The book is about the titular Solomon who falls in love with Mollia, a woman who recently lost her husband. The book revolves around the development of their romance but also a few hijinks. The book affirms a positive message with no eye turned to anything except its plot and the Jewish community.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>As an aside, I was raised in an Orthodox Jewish community (but haven’t participated in it since I was 13), and one nice thing about the book is that it felt authentic to me. I don’t have much experience with the Conservative community so I can\'t swear to its authenticity, but this book conveyed the hospitality and friendliness of the Jewish community.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'Solomon the Accountant - Edward M. Knauss', '', 'inherit', 'closed', 'closed', '', '199-revision-v1', '', '', '2019-12-20 02:22:33', '2019-12-20 02:22:33', '', 199, 'https://benyakiredits.com/uncategorized/199-revision-v1/', 0, 'revision', '', 0),
(202, 2, '2019-12-20 02:22:58', '2019-12-20 02:22:58', '<!-- wp:paragraph {\"fontSize\":\"large\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-large-font-size\">Rating: 5 stars</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>I read this self-proclaimed novella in a small break before my next hold is ready at the library, and I was very happy with my choice!</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It’s a Robin Hood fable but with frogs. That’s about all you need to know, though I don’t mind going into more detail. The story is great and a nice little distraction. The puns are lots of fun too. If I had my druthers, I’d make more of them, but I’m not the writer, and the writer took great care with this story. Though the novella is written tongue-in-cheek, it’s family friendly and reminded me of the funny, plucky Robin Hood stories I’d been raised with.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It’s a good, earnest work and deserving of respect. There’s a lot of flim-flam in writing, and this has none of it. The author wanted to tell a story, and he told one, one that I quite enjoyed.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'Ribbit Hood - Edward Van Winkle', '', 'inherit', 'closed', 'closed', '', '192-revision-v1', '', '', '2019-12-20 02:22:58', '2019-12-20 02:22:58', '', 192, 'https://benyakiredits.com/uncategorized/192-revision-v1/', 0, 'revision', '', 0),
(203, 2, '2019-12-20 02:24:37', '2019-12-20 02:24:37', '<!-- wp:paragraph {\"fontSize\":\"large\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-large-font-size\">Rating: 5 stars</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The good: The story is another Olive and Violet Bright adventure, quick moving, educational and entertaining.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The bad: Judith and Jack aren’t to be found in the story, but the story makes it up with new characters.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The long: The third book of the series outlines some expectations for what we can see in the future. By that I mean that all three books are different. It’s the shortest so far, and it’s set when the Brights and friends have grown up. One book is set in England while they were little, one is ambiguous in Hong Kong, and this is near Tahiti as adults. I like how this is done, that it allows different stories to be told while invoking at the same time a certain amount of familiarity.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Anyway, onto the book at hand. The gang are all working at the Pacific Research Institute, a means of uniting the many islands of that region. It is the inaugural mission, and Olive and Violet Bright work with local populations to figure out the mystery behind the resurfacing of an ancient blight, the beautiful Water Orchid. It’s consuming enormous quantities of water and is going to destroy the environment and the food chain of the Cocoanut Palm Island.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The book, much like its predecessors, spends a great amount of time educating us about the ways of life of Tahiti and similar cultures. The author also frequently uses native words and language to explain the situation. It’s an impulse I greatly appreciate because I know that it would be impossible to understand Italian culture and way of life without the language. The ultimate message is one about the mistakes of colonialism, the idiocy of the ‘noble savage’ stereotype and the power of unity. It is a great read, but it ends fast. I look forward to the future books in the series.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'The Water Orchids of Cocoanut Palm Island - Gloria T. August', '', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', 'the-water-orchids', '', '', '2021-03-13 04:44:50', '2021-03-13 04:44:50', '', 0, 'https://benyakiredits.com/?p=203', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(204, 2, '2019-12-20 02:24:37', '2019-12-20 02:24:37', '<!-- wp:paragraph {\"fontSize\":\"large\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-large-font-size\">Rating: 5 stars</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'The Water Orchids of Cocoanut Palm Island - Gloria T. August', '', 'inherit', 'closed', 'closed', '', '203-revision-v1', '', '', '2019-12-20 02:24:37', '2019-12-20 02:24:37', '', 203, 'https://benyakiredits.com/uncategorized/203-revision-v1/', 0, 'revision', '', 0),
