 -->\n<p>The number one objective of most traditional game is making it fun to overcome the obstacles created by the rules to accomplish your goal. For video games fun actually takes a back seat to the point of the creator (because the game is abstract, as I said, and any rules are arbitrary rather than physically imposed, such as the inability to run at the speed of light).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>I’m going to take an extreme example of how the beautiful takes a back seat to the point and not in terms of video games: Picasso’s Guernica. Is it pretty? No. You may like it aesthetically, but that’s not supposed to be its point. It’s about the horrors of war, not an impressionist landscape. Now fun or the point’s the primary goal of every well designed game, but back in the day it wasn’t. I’m thinking of the 90s, where supposed artistic vision overwrote gameplay and game design. Obviously, I’m poo-pooing that, not because I despise artistic vision but because it’s fundamentally misunderstanding the medium in which the artist is operating.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>I’ll give some examples to illustrate my point from something I was recently thinking about: Command and Conquer, where you build an army and harvest resources to do so. The resources are poisonous… what does that mean? It starts off with your army, which is basically modern armies (of the 90s) but a little futuristic) so it consists of infantry, tanks and planes. If infantry walks on top of the poison they get hurt. Also when infantry is shot they dive and take less damage but walk slower. There’s no manual way to control that. It makes sense in the game world, but does it make it fun? Or, more importantly, what’s the point of it? Is it about the disposability of humans? How generals have little regard for those under them or the chaos of war? But you can still command those soldiers to stand over your poisonous resources until they die pointlessly. The game doesn’t dwell on such ideas or have anything else that supports such interpretations. It’s incidental, not intentional, and isolated, not common. Both of those things are the opposite of what an artist should want. One simple ting they could do was given the individual soldiers more personality, and you command them to their death in spite of it. And any technological limitation should have been worked into the greater message.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>So what’s a good counterexample from a similar game? Starcraft! It’s very similar in that you are a commander over an army and have to gather resources to do so. It’s far in the future. There are humans, but it weaponizes the amount you’re supposed to be apathetic about those you command. It encourages you to treat the situation entirely arbitrary like The Art of War.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>I’m just going to go on a rant about  Ready Player One (okay, I haven\'t read it or seen it, but if you know me I despise it). As far as I understand it, the virtual reality people go into allow them to do anything. Much like the book itself, it’s misunderstanding art as a whole. An artist accomplishes their goal because of their constraints and because of their inabilities to do everything. Otherwise they’d hand you a blank canvas and say it’s the best they’ll ever do because it could show everything. If, for example, someone is showing you a religious work that is really supposed to be about human capacity of everything, that’s not what it’s saying. On the most basic level, humans are mortal, and it takes time to create anything. The artist is closer to death by producing it and so everything, because of human existence, cannot be perfect. As a side note, a game being an accurate representation of reality (or some sort of paradise/utopia) doesn’t mean it’s artistically bereft, just that its message has to rely upon what is failing (such as the inability for humans to transcend like the Buddha). \'Everything’s great!’ isn’t a message.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true,\"start\":2} -->\n<ol start=\"2\"><li>What’s special about Bloodborne</li></ol>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Well, I like Bloodborne so much because it deftly combines its message with its gameplay. To explain that, the very first thing I need to explain are  the limitations. You see it on a screen and use a controller to interact. What the game is creating uses more or less a computer to output information, effectively an artificial mind with one small job to do, which is the interactivity. The developer of the game’s question this is: how to use your controller to represent you. It’s actually not too complicated. By now it’s become ingrained in people who play what buttons do what. I’m not going to get into the technical aspects of it, but this is probably the least important part. You have a few buttons that are readily accessible and a stick that lets the character move where you want.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Onto how the game plays. Your perspective is from behind that of a person. The game happens in real times, and there is no way to stop it. You command the person to move, to talk (rarely) and how to hit things. The commands, at least the basics, are very simple. You only rely on two or three buttons most times. You can move around your guy or your camera (along a circle with its axis being your character). The message is this: there isn’t anything too complicated you can do (at least on a fundamental level), and the value of your actions is based on timing. You choose between nothing, defense or attacking when facing your foes. And when you don’t, you have no meaningful way of interacting with anyone besides the binary of non-interaction or interaction.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If I like it so much, it has to have a story then, right? How can it be strong without any sort of meaningful interaction if the medium is about that very thing? It reflects how the story is not important whatsoever to playing the game through on a surface level of victory conditions. You can go through the game and quite easily not learn what’s going on in any meaningful way. It’s all subtle, not directly said or stated in menus on unimportant text. There is a world out there, and it’s rich, but it doesn’t ask you to discover it. You have to want to see more. That incidentally is a mechanic, that the more you are exposed to the world, the more your constitution fails. But it\'s secondary and not too important.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>What makes Bloodborne art? The game is a metaphor for human existence. There is little choice (you either kill or be killed and you must progress in a linear way to get to the end); the greater, true narratives are unsaid (such as there are only a handful of companies that control virtually ever consumer product, so even if you buy an ‘ethically-made’ product from some alternative brand you’re still supporting a corporation that engages in unethical practices in its other sub-brands), and the only ones explained are the lesser, easier ones (such as you work to earn money and then you pay that for goods); and absolutely no one knows the entire picture.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true,\"start\":3} -->\n<ol start=\"3\"><li>American Bloodborne?</li></ol>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The story, what it actually says, is strikingly similar to America right now! By that I mean the details you have to hunt and peck for. In your fighting, you’ll occasionally (or often) get hurt, and you inject strange blood to recover. It is, in the introduction and its exposition, the essential plot of the story. People come from afar to partake in the special healing properties of a blood transfusion from Yharnam (the city you find yourself in).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Well, things quickly go downhill when you wake up, and the city’s in chaos. You go about and investigate, fighting your way through a mob of people who want to kill you. They’re on the hunt of beasts, you see, and will attack anyone in their way. In your first insight into the truth of the world, you find out as you kill them you can more or less make yourself better by using their blood to empower yourself.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Soon, you discover that the blood that you consume is what transforms you into a beast. Those who hunt the beasts and use the blood vials to keep themselves alive are dooming themselves to become what they hunt (you can supply the Nietzsche quote here). And the healing church, the organization that creates the blood, is obviously covering this up because they want everyone to transform into monstrosities. They even went so far as to have half their town burned to the ground to hide the truth. For you to prosper others must suffer, and that what makes you able to live longer causes you to lose your humanity (that isn\'t a gameplay mechanic, which is one of the game\'s biggest flaws).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Going forward you make your way up the strata of the healing church, only to find it led by a figurehead. You investigate it more, and things spiral completely out of control. The real leadership is long dead and another plane of existence, completely uninterested in bettering the situation. They’re worshipping gods that cannot comprehend our reality, and their slightest interaction causes humans to either become beasts or hideous abominations. And the people who empower the healing church are encouraging this in order to convert everyone else into worshippers of these strange gods. For the beings themselves or those who have come close to apotheosis, their actions are fundamentally incompatible with rational thought or what normal people need or want. There isn’t even a man behind the curtain. There is only nothing or, at best, a callousness that will inadvertently destroy you and everything you care about.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>I hope you understand the allegory.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'American Bloodborne', '', 'inherit', 'closed', 'closed', '', '607-revision-v1', '', '', '2020-06-03 20:18:54', '2020-06-03 20:18:54', '', 607, 'https://benyakiredits.com/uncategorized/607-revision-v1/', 0, 'revision', '', 0),
(656, 2, '2020-08-03 23:57:57', '2020-08-03 23:57:57', '<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>I want to share with you my story from 2016. After spending hundreds (thousands) of hours, nights and every spare moment I had, I produced my first book. I was so sure I had created something universal and something that made myself proud.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Well, if that\'s how I start, you know the answer. Few people liked it, finding it weird, incomprehensible or badly written. I\'ve spent the last four years of my life working on that a lot because, unfortunately, I fell in love with writing.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>And that leads me to the purpose of this website, where I do three things: I talk about the books I write, I edit, and I create a blog (for both book reviews and my thoughts, both sections called pretty much that). If you want to hear about my books (one for now but more are coming), go to the aptly-named section \"my books\" under the side bar (WIP). Or read my blogs if that\'s what you\'re after. However, if you are interested in my editing services, read below or skip to the chase and go to <a href=\"https://benyakiredits.com/services/\">Services</a>.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>So, a little more about my first book (as I thought): My passion and my being soaked every word. I believed it was ready so I shared it with some friends and family. You eagerly expected them to say something nice or just not too harsh. Some constructive criticism would be nice, too.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>However, the feedback wasn\'t what I was expecting. My book was fine, good really, just not what they liked, or there are all these little problems: a inconsistencies in how I used numbers, typos, the flow of the sentences, petty arguments about something that was unrelated to the book and so on. As much as I cherished the people who talked to me, I felt like it didn\'t get to the heart of the matter. So I studied, read and spent time to figure out all these things. I learned two very important lessons: 1. each person is good at some or a lot of things but not everything and 2. for that reason we all need someone else\'s eyes on our work.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Once you have perused the free resources out there, you will find yourself at a similar point and just need eyes on your work. So why pay for what you can get free elsewhere? I do three things, professionally and with a schedule we agree to:: line editing, copy editing and proofreading. You can read about what they mean <a href=\"https://nybookeditors.com/2015/01/copyediting-vs-line-editing/\">here</a> and <a href=\"https://www.scribendi.com/advice/what_is_proofreading.en.html\">here</a>. I can also do a small amount of developmental editing, but it is not my specialty.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For a short time, I will edit the first 20,000 words of your work for free. No stipulations, but I would appreciate a kind word for a testimonial on my website.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For further information, go to <a href=\"https://benyakiredits.com/services/\">Services</a>.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'Author and Editor', '', 'inherit', 'closed', 'closed', '', '40-revision-v1', '', '', '2020-08-03 23:57:57', '2020-08-03 23:57:57', '', 40, 'https://benyakiredits.com/uncategorized/40-revision-v1/', 0, 'revision', '', 0),
(644, 2, '2020-08-03 22:30:22', '2020-08-03 22:30:22', '<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>[mailerlite_form form_id=1]</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'Newsletter', '', 'publish', 'closed', 'closed', '', 'newsletter', '', '', '2021-03-13 08:18:31', '2021-03-13 08:18:31', '', 48, 'https://benyakiredits.com/?page_id=644', 0, 'page', '', 0),
(645, 2, '2020-08-03 22:30:03', '2020-08-03 22:30:03', '<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>[mailerlite_form form_id=1]</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'Newsletter', '', 'inherit', 'closed', 'closed', '', '644-revision-v1', '', '', '2020-08-03 22:30:03', '2020-08-03 22:30:03', '', 644, 'https://benyakiredits.com/uncategorized/644-revision-v1/', 0, 'revision', '', 0);
INSERT INTO `_STA_posts` (`ID`, `post_author`, `post_date`, `post_date_gmt`, `post_content`, `post_title`, `post_excerpt`, `post_status`, `comment_status`, `ping_status`, `post_password`, `post_name`, `to_ping`, `pinged`, `post_modified`, `post_modified_gmt`, `post_content_filtered`, `post_parent`, `guid`, `menu_order`, `post_type`, `post_mime_type`, `comment_count`) VALUES
(998, 2, '2021-10-11 03:07:55', '2021-10-11 03:07:55', '<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 id=\"toc\">Table of Content </h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul><li><a href=\"#the-basics\">The Basics</a></li><li><a href=\"#writing\">Writing</a></li><li><a href=\"#programming\">Programming</a></li></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Now that it\'s been more than a year since this latest journey began, I wanna talk about it. Specifically, about how my previous profession relates to it. When I\'ve worked on my Recovering Grandeur project, I had this idea: that I was going to create something with meaning, something that could at least vaguely be called art. The idea for this post came from me trying to think through that comment. So I\'ll start from the beginning and hopefully proceed to somewhere with some meaning.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 id=\"the-basics\">The Basics</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If you\'re ever communicate, you\'re going to express something. And if you\'re going to express anything at all, you are doing so through some medium. There are a bunch of linguistic theories and names I could toss out here, about usage versus meaning or some such thing. But I\'ll skip over that and just say this: people can\'t read your mind. You have to express your meaning something, and my first sentence just means \'express your thoughts in some way.\'</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It can happen many ways (I assure you): talking to a friend, writing a blog post on the internet, speaking at a lecture, yawning when you\'re tired, drawing a picture... you get the idea, surely. This is the basics of modern academic study, putting everything into boxes so it can be defined, examined and speculated about. When you were a kid and had to deliver a book report, you probably didn\'t spend any time cogitating about public address (nor about the word cogitating). When you hang out with your buddy, you probably don\'t think about the formality of what you\'re saying, the limitations of speech and the value of present context.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>One other thing to keep in mind, consequently, is that there are always exactly two parties in any communication. There is the speaker and the receiver. If either of these are lacking, it\'s not communication. You have to talk to someone. Then you have third parties, but they\'re secondary (okay, that was a horrible pun), as in they don\'t have any impact on the communication and don\'t really matter. A book, for example, is communication between the author and the audience. It\'s disguised as being addressed to an unknown second party (usually) and the reader is actually a third party, but that\'s wrong. If you\'re reading something, you are the person I am writing this for.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Let\'s clear up some misconceptions.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul><li>Even if you\'re reading something hundreds of years after the author died, you are communicating. The author intended for you to make you ponder their words and reconsider your world.</li><li>What that message is and its meaning is independent of what the author wanted. It depends on you, the reader, to decide it. Death of an author, baby.</li><li>To connect with that last point, meaning is defined a lot by context. For example, when a book gives an amount in money. A lot of Murakami books will do this, where an item will cost a certain amount of yen. As someone who not only doesn\'t know Japanese culture and much less Japanese culture of a decade ago (depending on the book), what does it mean? Is it a lot? A little? Every time a monetary price for anything at all is in a book, it becomes hard to relate. Even American books written a decade ago have a greatly different meaning in terms of money. Another example is when I read Flowers for Algernon. I read it while I was in rehab after my coma. It meant something special for me in that exact moment that it wouldn\'t have in any other moment, even if I still think it\'s a really good book. Or, in the other direction, something like The White Man\'s Burden looks foolish, paternalistic, infantilizing, incredibly racist and like a parody of itself given a modern context, at least it should. If you don\'t think Rudyard Kipling is a fucking monster or a polite wording thereof after reading it, I don\'t think you should ever read anything I have to say. I don\'t have anything to say to you, and so what\'s the point in trying to communicate with me?</li><li>To not speak to anyone isn\'t communication. If you\'re at a party and the person you\'re talking to zones you out and just nods politely, there is no point to talking. I\'m not giving a justification for acting like a douchebag and pointing this out when you feel like someone isn\'t listening to you. In fact, if someone\'s blowing you off at a party and not paying attention to what you\'re saying, it\'s usually communicating something: that you are not someone worth talking to, at least in that moment.</li><li>The burden is on the speaker and not the audience to create a meaningful statement or the foundation for it.</li></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Moving on, I want to talk about the limitations of a medium. You see, a medium isn\'t defined by what it can do but by what it can\'t do. Speech is defined mostly by its context and its lack of portability. If you\'ve ever heard \'you had to be there\' in response to someone repeating a story or something, that\'s an example of what I\'m talking about. Speech has a much larger dependency on the immediate context or the reconstruction thereof. Take the Iliad and the Odyssey, or the Bible (Old Testament or the New Testament). There are a lot of other ones such as Beowulf, which were handed down from oral traditions then later transcribed into written work. If you\'ve ever read any of these, woof.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Communication through a medium is an abstraction of reality. What has constantly happened throughout all of human history is a constant battle between abstraction and technicality versus realism and communicability. Every medium of communication requires some level of meditation on how to communicate in that medium, but sometimes people dwell in that so much the message is garbled or requires a lot of thinking.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That last sentence may make no sense on first glance, but let me give you an example. You know, instinctively, where I should capitalize and use punctuation. Where I don\'t use it correctly is either me failing to do it correctly (which means it\'s incorrect, even if you understand the message) or a stylistic choice (usually to point something out).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If looked as a gradient, speaking informally is the medium that requires the least abstraction to communicate effectively. If you don\'t believe me, record yourself talking to your friends or family for 10 minutes and count every time you stop a sentence or word halfway through, resume it, change what you\'re saying, change the sentence structure, etc. Because there\'s very little abstraction, it is rare to fail to communicate in speaking. Despite what I said, failures and what stops them from happening can be very complicated and is basically sociolinguistics.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>On the other end of the field is programming, at least of the writing of this article. I\'ll get into this, but there is no effective communication in programming without adhering to extremely strict rules.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 id=\"writing\">Writing</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It may come to a surprise to you, but I have really good command of the English language. I know how to get my message across, but I choose to write like this. Like this being more of a colloquial style like how I would talk, at least if I had an idea in mind of how I were to write.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It\'s actually an important distinction to make. Writing has been subject to the same discussion about abstraction as everything else. On the one extreme, writing is a thin veneer over people speaking (c.f. almost any poorly edited bestseller). On the other end, you have something like Cicero\'s <em>De Oratore</em>. I\'m sure there are a bunch of great examples, but I tend not to read them because they\'re a real pain and no fun at all. They delight in the complexities of grammar over being easy to understand.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>I guess that segues into what I actually kinda want to talk about. There are some technical skills involved with writing. Fortunately, you\'ve picked up (most of) them. A quarter is your conversational skills. Or more, really, if you are good at talking. Then there is some arbitrary knowledge, which you\'re going to spend a long time in school learning. Then you learn to make a point. And this is something you have to think about less when in a conversation, but in writing, you have time to think about what you want to say. Most importantly, you learn about why you should communicate. The reason why this is so much more important when you\'re writing over when you\'re talking (which writing is supposed to imitate, right?) is that you get to carefully craft your message over time. It\'s less stream-of-consciousness, or it should be. And if it\'s more stream-of-consciousness, it has to be for a reason.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>But remember, a medium reveals itself in its limitations. So what are the limitations? Two things:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul><li>1. writing is limited by the language it\'s written in. All that time and guile serves only to hide how arbitrary language is. It has little or no connection to reality, other than the limits of human (or personal) vocal anatomy. One thing that I want to hammer home is that grammar doesn\'t tell you how to do anything in a language. Rather, a good grammar of a language is one that describes what speakers usually do.</li><li>2. Writing can\'t do anything beyond what you\'re reading it on. It can easily be incorporated into something else, such as a movie or an inscription on a sculpture. But those things are fundamentally different from writing as a pure form. Everything is a metaphor, and writing can never be beyond it. People can interpret it and act upon it, but in of itself, it doesn\'t do anything.</li></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 id=\"programming\">Programming</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Imagine you became a god and got to remake language and all of creation (aside from a few base rules), but you were still stupid you. You aren\'t omniscient (I presume). So you start off with the base rules, and they\'re really arcane. First off, there\'s no letters and just 0s and 1s. You learn the rules then create layers of abstraction on top of that so you can do something easy like say \"add 2 and 5\" instead of whatever thing you can\'t actually read. Then you\'re get to the point of saying \"what\'s the point of adding 2 and 5?\"</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Just an aside, a lot of the gods are misanthropes. And they each think \"but this abstraction sucks! Let\'s write a better one!\" And no one does. All their abstractions suck in one way or another. They\'re all unnatural (no matter how much they do or don\'t try) and artificial. There is no beauty or poetry in any of their abstractions. Please note: I\'m not asking for it to be beautiful. I just want to make that distinction, tha tit isn\'t.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>So that\'s a summary of the limitations. Effectively none, except you\'re limited to a computer. With miniature computers getting into most everything, well, you have less of that. But there is a problem. Programming has no art to it because nothing in programming is a metaphor.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:benyakirsplugins/syntax-highlighter {\"lang\":\"python\",\"code\":\"def fib(n):\\n   return 1 if n __L_ANGLE_BRACKET__= 2 else fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)\",\"theme\":\"prism\"} /-->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That\'s probably the simplest implementation of an algorithm to find a Fibonacci number at a certain index, and every single of those words do something important. I could tell you exactly what they do. There are a few optional things I could add, but you get the idea. The point being here is that everything in programming is an instruction that our computer carries out to the best of its abilities according to the context we place it in.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This is something that I spent more time than ever was deserved thinking about. Because in writing, what you have to say and how you say it are both important. And in programming, there are really only two metrics: what you accomplish and how effectively you accomplish it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Let me give you some examples. I\'m just going to go with my latest project, <a href=\"https://github.com/benyakirten/benyakir-writes\">benyakir-writes</a>. I wanted to implement search functionality. On the WordPress version of the site, there\'s a search bar. You enter some text, and violà, some results. I wanted that on my new blog too, so what\'s wrong? I just slap together something and copy what I already have, and it should work.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Let me tell you why it doesn\'t work. So let me step back to when I said programming was acting like god. But it\'s so complicated with you having to micromanage so many things that you create abstractions? To cook an egg, you have to figure out what fire is, how to light a stove, how to put the pan on the stove, how to crack the egg, how to leave it be until it\'s cooked. Once you\'ve figured out how to cook an egg once, you just say \"let an egg be cooked\", and it\'s done. Well, except you need to figure out what to do if someone gives you a quail egg. Or what if it\'s calcified? What if it\'s actually just a rock in the shape of an egg?</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>So let\'s just skip ahead a bunch of everything and say there\'s the internet. There\'s already a bunch of complicated things that happened before the internet and to allow it to happen, but we\'re just skipping ahead to the internet. We have to decide how things are going to get shared between each computer. We could effectively send anything we want over the internet. That is, in fact, what we do. But what good is sending something over? So what?</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It has to be understood. So all of our gentlepeople computers (and their touchers) have come to an agreement. If you have an explicit contract with another computer, whatever goes. If you are reaching out to someone on the web, you do it through an internet browser, and things happen in a strict way. There are a few formalities like HTTP headers, and then the server you reach out to sends you the information. That information comes in one of five formats: heaps of data (like downloading a file or a picture), HTML (a general description of the page structure), CSS (a bunch of descriptors of how to decorate/arrange the HTML), JavaScript (the meat and bones of everything that\'s going on) and now WebAssembly, something that\'s more complicated and I\'m too lazy to go into right now.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'Programming vs. Writing', '', 'inherit', 'closed', 'closed', '', '978-revision-v1', '', '', '2021-10-11 03:07:55', '2021-10-11 03:07:55', '', 978, 'https://benyakiredits.com/?p=998', 0, 'revision', '', 0),
(997, 2, '2021-10-11 02:45:10', '2021-10-11 02:45:10', '<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 id=\"toc\">Table of Content </h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul><li><a href=\"#the-basics\">The Basics</a></li><li><a href=\"#writing\">Writing</a></li><li><a href=\"#programming\">Programming</a></li></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Now that it\'s been more than a year since this latest journey began, I wanna talk about it. Specifically, about how my previous profession relates to it. When I\'ve worked on my Recovering Grandeur project, I had this idea: that I was going to create something with meaning, something that could at least vaguely be called art. The idea for this post came from me trying to think through that comment. So I\'ll start from the beginning and hopefully proceed to somewhere with some meaning.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 id=\"the-basics\">The Basics</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If you\'re ever communicate, you\'re going to express something. And if you\'re going to express anything at all, you are doing so through some medium. There are a bunch of linguistic theories and names I could toss out here, about usage versus meaning or some such thing. But I\'ll skip over that and just say this: people can\'t read your mind. You have to express your meaning something, and my first sentence just means \'express your thoughts in some way.\'</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It can happen many ways (I assure you): talking to a friend, writing a blog post on the internet, speaking at a lecture, yawning when you\'re tired, drawing a picture... you get the idea, surely. This is the basics of modern academic study, putting everything into boxes so it can be defined, examined and speculated about. When you were a kid and had to deliver a book report, you probably didn\'t spend any time cogitating about public address (nor about the word cogitating). When you hang out with your buddy, you probably don\'t think about the formality of what you\'re saying, the limitations of speech and the value of present context.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>One other thing to keep in mind, consequently, is that there are always exactly two parties in any communication. There is the speaker and the receiver. If either of these are lacking, it\'s not communication. You have to talk to someone. Then you have third parties, but they\'re secondary (okay, that was a horrible pun), as in they don\'t have any impact on the communication and don\'t really matter. A book, for example, is communication between the author and the audience. It\'s disguised as being addressed to an unknown second party (usually) and the reader is actually a third party, but that\'s wrong. If you\'re reading something, you are the person I am writing this for.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Let\'s clear up some misconceptions.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul><li>Even if you\'re reading something hundreds of years after the author died, you are communicating. The author intended for you to make you ponder their words and reconsider your world.</li><li>What that message is and its meaning is independent of what the author wanted. It depends on you, the reader, to decide it. Death of an author, baby.</li><li>To connect with that last point, meaning is defined a lot by context. For example, when a book gives an amount in money. A lot of Murakami books will do this, where an item will cost a certain amount of yen. As someone who not only doesn\'t know Japanese culture and much less Japanese culture of a decade ago (depending on the book), what does it mean? Is it a lot? A little? Every time a monetary price for anything at all is in a book, it becomes hard to relate. Even American books written a decade ago have a greatly different meaning in terms of money. Another example is when I read Flowers for Algernon. I read it while I was in rehab after my coma. It meant something special for me in that exact moment that it wouldn\'t have in any other moment, even if I still think it\'s a really good book. Or, in the other direction, something like The White Man\'s Burden looks foolish, paternalistic, infantilizing, incredibly racist and like a parody of itself given a modern context, at least it should. If you don\'t think Rudyard Kipling is a fucking monster or a polite wording thereof after reading it, I don\'t think you should ever read anything I have to say. I don\'t have anything to say to you, and so what\'s the point in trying to communicate with me?</li><li>To not speak to anyone isn\'t communication. If you\'re at a party and the person you\'re talking to zones you out and just nods politely, there is no point to talking. I\'m not giving a justification for acting like a douchebag and pointing this out when you feel like someone isn\'t listening to you. In fact, if someone\'s blowing you off at a party and not paying attention to what you\'re saying, it\'s usually communicating something: that you are not someone worth talking to, at least in that moment.</li><li>The burden is on the speaker and not the audience to create a meaningful statement or the foundation for it.</li></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Moving on, I want to talk about the limitations of a medium. You see, a medium isn\'t defined by what it can do but by what it can\'t do. Speech is defined mostly by its context and its lack of portability. If you\'ve ever heard \'you had to be there\' in response to someone repeating a story or something, that\'s an example of what I\'m talking about. Speech has a much larger dependency on the immediate context or the reconstruction thereof. Take the Iliad and the Odyssey, or the Bible (Old Testament or the New Testament). There are a lot of other ones such as Beowulf, which were handed down from oral traditions then later transcribed into written work. If you\'ve ever read any of these, woof.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Communication through a medium is an abstraction of reality. What has constantly happened throughout all of human history is a constant battle between abstraction and technicality versus realism and communicability. Every medium of communication requires some level of meditation on how to communicate in that medium, but sometimes people dwell in that so much the message is garbled or requires a lot of thinking.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That last sentence may make no sense on first glance, but let me give you an example. You know, instinctively, where I should capitalize and use punctuation. Where I don\'t use it correctly is either me failing to do it correctly (which means it\'s incorrect, even if you understand the message) or a stylistic choice (usually to point something out).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If looked as a gradient, speaking informally is the medium that requires the least abstraction to communicate effectively. If you don\'t believe me, record yourself talking to your friends or family for 10 minutes and count every time you stop a sentence or word halfway through, resume it, change what you\'re saying, change the sentence structure, etc. Because there\'s very little abstraction, it is rare to fail to communicate in speaking. Despite what I said, failures and what stops them from happening can be very complicated and is basically sociolinguistics.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>On the other end of the field is programming, at least of the writing of this article. I\'ll get into this, but there is no effective communication in programming without adhering to extremely strict rules.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 id=\"writing\">Writing</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It may come to a surprise to you, but I have really good command of the English language. I know how to get my message across, but I choose to write like this. Like this being more of a colloquial style like how I would talk, at least if I had an idea in mind of how I were to write.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It\'s actually an important distinction to make. Writing has been subject to the same discussion about abstraction as everything else. On the one extreme, writing is a thin veneer over people speaking (c.f. almost any poorly edited bestseller). On the other end, you have something like Cicero\'s <em>De Oratore</em>. I\'m sure there are a bunch of great examples, but I tend not to read them because they\'re a real pain and no fun at all. They delight in the complexities of grammar over being easy to understand.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>I guess that segues into what I actually kinda want to talk about. There are some technical skills involved with writing. Fortunately, you\'ve picked up (most of) them. A quarter is your conversational skills. Or more, really, if you are good at talking. Then there is some arbitrary knowledge, which you\'re going to spend a long time in school learning. Then you learn to make a point. And this is something you have to think about less when in a conversation, but in writing, you have time to think about what you want to say. Most importantly, you learn about why you should communicate. The reason why this is so much more important when you\'re writing over when you\'re talking (which writing is supposed to imitate, right?) is that you get to carefully craft your message over time. It\'s less stream-of-consciousness, or it should be. And if it\'s more stream-of-consciousness, it has to be for a reason.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>But remember, a medium reveals itself in its limitations. So what are the limitations? Two things:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul><li>1. writing is limited by the language it\'s written in. All that time and guile serves only to hide how arbitrary language is. It has little or no connection to reality, other than the limits of human (or personal) vocal anatomy. One thing that I want to hammer home is that grammar doesn\'t tell you how to do anything in a language. Rather, a good grammar of a language is one that describes what speakers usually do.</li><li>2. Writing can\'t do anything beyond what you\'re reading it on. It can easily be incorporated into something else, such as a movie or an inscription on a sculpture. But those things are fundamentally different from writing as a pure form. Everything is a metaphor, and writing can never be beyond it. People can interpret it and act upon it, but in of itself, it doesn\'t do anything.</li></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 id=\"programming\">Programming</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Imagine you became a god and got to remake language and all of creation (aside from a few base rules), but you were still stupid you. You aren\'t omniscient (I presume). So you start off with the base rules, and they\'re really arcane. First off, there\'s no letters and just 0s and 1s. You learn the rules then create layers of abstraction on top of that so you can do something easy like say \"add 2 and 5\" instead of whatever thing you can\'t actually read. Then you\'re get to the point of saying \"what\'s the point of adding 2 and 5?\"</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Just an aside, a lot of the gods are misanthropes. And they each think \"but this abstraction sucks! Let\'s write a better one!\" And no one does. All their abstractions suck in one way or another. They\'re all unnatural (no matter how much they do or don\'t try) and artificial. There is no beauty or poetry in any of their abstractions. Please note: I\'m not asking for it to be beautiful. I just want to make that distinction, tha tit isn\'t.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>So that\'s a summary of the limitations. Effectively none, except you\'re limited to a computer. With miniature computers getting into most everything, well, you have less of that. But there is a problem. Programming has no art to it because nothing in programming is a metaphor.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:benyakirsplugins/syntax-highlighter {\"lang\":\"python\",\"code\":\"def fib(n):\\n   return 1 if n __L_ANGLE_BRACKET__= 2 else fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)\",\"theme\":\"prism\"} /-->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That\'s probably the simplest implementation of an algorithm to find a Fibonacci number at a certain index, and every single of those words do something important. I could tell you exactly what they do. There are a few optional things I could add, but you get the idea. The point being here is that everything in programming is an instruction that our computer carries out to the best of its abilities according to the context we place it in.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This is something that I spent more time than ever was deserved thinking about. Because in writing, what you have to say and how you say it are both important. And in programming, there are really only two metrics: what you accomplish and how effectively you accomplish it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Let me give you some examples. I\'m just going to go with my latest project, <a href=\"https://github.com/benyakirten/benyakir-writes\">benyakir-writes</a>. I wanted to implement search functionality. On the WordPress version of the site, there\'s a search bar. You enter some text, and violà, some results. I wanted that on my new blog too, so what\'s wrong? I just slap together something and copy what I already have, and it should work.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Let me tell you why it doesn\'t work. So let me step back to when I said programming was acting like god. But it\'s so complicated with you having to micromanage so many things that you create abstractions? To cook an egg, you have to figure out what fire is, how to light a stove, how to put the pan on the stove, how to crack the egg, how to leave it be until it\'s cooked. Once you\'ve figured out how to cook an egg once, you just say \"let an egg be cooked\", and it\'s done. Except you need to figure out what to do if someone gives you a quail egg. Or what if it\'s calcified? What if it\'s actually just a rock in the shape of an egg?</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>So let\'s just skip ahead a bunch of everything and say there\'s the internet. There\'s already a bunch of complicated things that happened before the internet and to allow it to happen, but we\'re just skipping ahead to the internet. We have to decide how things are going to get shared between each computer. We could effectively send anything we want over the internet. That is, in fact, what we do. But what good is sending something over? So what?</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It has to be understood. So all of our gentlepeople computers (and their touchers) have come to an agreement. If you have an explicit contract with another computer, whatever goes. If you are reaching out to someone on the web, you do it through an internet browser, and things happen in a strict way. There are a few formalities like HTTP headers, and then the server you reach out to sends you the information. That information comes in one of five formats: heaps of data (like downloading a file or a picture), HTML (a general description of the page structure), CSS (a bunch of descriptors of how to decorate/arrange the HTML), JavaScript (the meat and bones of everything that\'s going on) and now WebAssembly, something that\'s more complicated and I\'m too lazy to go into right now.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'Programming vs. Writing', '', 'inherit', 'closed', 'closed', '', '978-revision-v1', '', '', '2021-10-11 02:45:10', '2021-10-11 02:45:10', '', 978, 'https://benyakiredits.com/?p=997', 0, 'revision', '', 0),
