th antisemitic ideas. They rely on simplistic truths, ignoring words and bending their meaning in order to, as Belbo writes in a private document on his computer, absolve them of their inabilities. If the conspiracy theories are true, if the Plan is true, then they can\'t be at fault for any of their failings. It is because of someone conspiring against them, not because of factors beyond their control.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>When we do get to the more traditional narrational aspects of the book, the story isn\'t too developed or long, so for that part, it\'s probably not too great. The characters, however, are all very interesting and dynamic. Even if that\'s all I read the book for, I would\'ve been happy about it. However, caveat emptor, it\'s what I call a European narrative. It has a little missing off the beginning and end of the story, so if you want those, then you might be disappointed. I wasn\'t.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>I enjoyed the book and hope you do too.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'Foucault\'s Pendulum - Umberto Eco', '', 'inherit', 'closed', 'closed', '', '628-revision-v1', '', '', '2020-07-18 17:55:26', '2020-07-18 17:55:26', '', 628, 'https://benyakiredits.com/uncategorized/628-revision-v1/', 0, 'revision', '', 0),
(630, 2, '2020-07-20 15:37:56', '2020-07-20 15:37:56', '<!-- wp:paragraph {\"fontSize\":\"large\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-large-font-size\">Rating: 4.5 stars</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>When I first realized the Friar’s Lantern was a Choose-Your-Own-Adventure, I thought it was a gimmick. And I tried to think of how an author could interact with such a medium to say something. As I read the book, I realized that it pretty much is how I would do exactly that. What I think is the most important is that the ability to choose is part of the message. It reminded me of Isaac Asimov’s I, Robot, which is a pretty large compliment from me.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The story begins with an applied version of Newcomb’s Paradox (which the author thankfully mentions at the end of the book), which is about how someone makes the choices they do. The format works very well because it calls on the reader to go through the process of realizing that the decisions they make aren’t as freely made or impactful as they each. This effect is at the base of the philosophical discussion posed by the book. But it goes even beyond that, and almost every event in the story is a meditation on predestination and free choice.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The main sub-plot that reinforces is the case of Dr. Solon. I won’t spoil anything, but the case is entirely dependent on whether he was responsible for his action or whether the action is a result of subconscious efforts and brain chemicals. Reinforcing this is also the time that the protagonist plays Three-card Monty, talking to an artificial intelligence (or maybe not), and others.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Now, I’ll talk about what I didn’t like as much in the book. I guess I’d talk about characters. It’s true that they aren’t entirely interesting, but I don’t know how much you can do in this type of format. The book is necessarily written in 2nd-person, and that makes it impossible to give too much flavor to the protagonist (you) because he or she is intentionally blank so the reader can fill in the shoes.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>My biggest problem actually stems from this (somewhat). In the first few chapters, the book has a lot of male gaze-type observations about women’s bodies and also what felt somewhat judgmental when it came to describing people in the courthouse. Besides me not liking it personally and finding it frankly off-putting, I think it’s an impediment to the readers of a CYOA book because they have to put themselves in the protagonist’s shoes.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'The Friar\'s Lantern - Greg Hickey', '', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', 'the-friars-lantern', '', '', '2021-03-09 20:30:14', '2021-03-09 20:30:14', '', 0, 'https://benyakiredits.com/?p=630', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(631, 2, '2020-07-20 15:37:07', '2020-07-20 15:37:07', '<!-- wp:paragraph {\"fontSize\":\"large\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-large-font-size\">Rating: 4.5 stars</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>When I first realized the Friar’s Lantern was a Choose-Your-Own-Adventure, I thought it was a gimmick. And I tried to think of how an author could interact with such a medium to say something. As I read the book, I realized that it pretty much is how I would do exactly that. What I think is the most important is that the ability to choose is part of the message. It reminded me of Isaac Asimov’s I, Robot, which is a pretty large compliment from me.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The story begins with an applied version of Newcomb’s Paradox (which the author thankfully mentions at the end of the book), which is about how someone makes the choices they do. The format works very well because it calls on the reader to go through the process of realizing that the decisions they make aren’t as freely made or impactful as they each. This effect is at the base of the philosophical discussion posed by the book. But it goes even beyond that, and almost every event in the story is a meditation on predestination and free choice.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The main sub-plot that reinforces is the case of Dr. Solon. I won’t spoil anything, but the case is entirely dependent on whether he was responsible for his action or whether the action is a result of subconscious efforts and brain chemicals. Reinforcing this is also the time that the protagonist plays Three-card Monty, talking to an artificial intelligence (or maybe not), and others.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Now, I’ll talk about what I didn’t like as much in the book. I guess I’d talk about characters. It’s true that they aren’t entirely interesting, but I don’t know how much you can do in this type of format. The book is necessarily written in 2nd-person, and that makes it impossible to give too much flavor to the protagonist (you) because he or she is intentionally blank so the reader can fill in the shoes.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>My biggest problem actually stems from this (somewhat). In the first few chapters, the book has a lot of male gaze-type observations about women’s bodies and also what felt somewhat judgmental when it came to describing people in the courthouse. Besides me not liking it personally and finding it frankly off-putting, I think it’s an impediment to the readers of a CYOA book because they have to put themselves in the protagonist’s shoes.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'The Friar\'s Lantern - Greg Hickey', '', 'inherit', 'closed', 'closed', '', '630-revision-v1', '', '', '2020-07-20 15:37:07', '2020-07-20 15:37:07', '', 630, 'https://benyakiredits.com/uncategorized/630-revision-v1/', 0, 'revision', '', 0),
(806, 2, '2021-03-13 06:18:13', '2021-03-13 06:18:13', '<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Stub for items on the front page</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'Front Page Stub', '', 'inherit', 'closed', 'closed', '', '40-revision-v1', '', '', '2021-03-13 06:18:13', '2021-03-13 06:18:13', '', 40, 'https://benyakiredits.com/?p=806', 0, 'revision', '', 0),
(695, 2, '2020-08-22 19:34:35', '2020-08-22 19:34:35', '<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>I want to share with you my story from 2016. After spending hundreds (thousands) of hours, nights and every spare moment I had, I produced my first book. I was so sure I had created something universal and something that made myself proud.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Well, if that\'s how I start, you know the answer. Few people liked it, finding it weird, incomprehensible or badly written. I\'ve spent the last four years of my life working on that a lot because, unfortunately, I fell in love with writing.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>And that leads me to the purpose of this website, where I do three things: I talk about the books I write, I edit, and I create a blog (for both book reviews and my thoughts, both sections called pretty much that). If you want to hear about my books (one for now but more are coming), go to the aptly-named <a href=\"https://benyakiredits.com/books/\">books</a> section. Or read my blogs if that\'s what you\'re after. However, if you are interested in my editing services, read below or skip to the chase and go to <a href=\"https://benyakiredits.com/services/\">Services</a>.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>So, a little more about my first book (as I thought): My passion and my being soaked every word. I believed it was ready so I shared it with some friends and family. You eagerly expected them to say something nice or just not too harsh. Some constructive criticism would be nice, too.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>However, the feedback wasn\'t what I was expecting. My book was fine, good really, just not what they liked, or there are all these little problems: a inconsistencies in how I used numbers, typos, the flow of the sentences, petty arguments about something that was unrelated to the book and so on. As much as I cherished the people who talked to me, I felt like it didn\'t get to the heart of the matter. So I studied, read and spent time to figure out all these things. I learned two very important lessons: 1. each person is good at some or a lot of things but not everything and 2. for that reason we all need someone else\'s eyes on our work.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Once you have perused the free resources out there, you will find yourself at a similar point and just need eyes on your work. So why pay for what you can get free elsewhere? I do three things, professionally and with a schedule we agree to:: line editing, copy editing and proofreading. You can read about what they mean <a href=\"https://nybookeditors.com/2015/01/copyediting-vs-line-editing/\">here</a> and <a href=\"https://www.scribendi.com/advice/what_is_proofreading.en.html\">here</a>. I can also do a small amount of developmental editing, but it is not my specialty.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For a short time, I will edit the first 20,000 words of your work for free. No stipulations, but I would appreciate a kind word for a testimonial on my website.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For further information on how to get started, go to <a href=\"https://benyakiredits.com/services/\">Services</a>.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'Author and Editor', '', 'inherit', 'closed', 'closed', '', '40-revision-v1', '', '', '2020-08-22 19:34:35', '2020-08-22 19:34:35', '', 40, 'https://benyakiredits.com/uncategorized/40-revision-v1/', 0, 'revision', '', 0),
(696, 2, '2020-08-24 16:31:18', '2020-08-24 16:31:18', '<!-- wp:paragraph {\"fontSize\":\"large\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-large-font-size\">Rating: 3.5 stars</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>I had this strange idea that I’d start to read some recently written science fiction to make sure that I don’t dislike all of it because I keep harping on how much I haven’t read anything written in the last ten years (okay, more than that, but let’s be generous) that I really like. Turns out this collection includes fantasy too, which doesn’t really bother me, since I’ve read some and not despised it. Turns out there’s only one real fantasy story in this collection, and it’s actually great.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>I had this idea that I’d talk about every story in the collection. I could do that, but I’ll just point out the low point (there’s only one), the few high points, and I’d not talk about the mediocre stories (of which there are many).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That’s really where the rating for this book came from. There are some outstanding or great stories, but they’re few and far between, and most of them aren’t really sci-fi or fantasy. Stories that don’t have a real point make up the majority of the book. There is really only three sci-fi stories I liked, which I’ll talk about later.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>First, the lowest point! It’s the very first story. It’s horrible to begin like this, but <em>Rivers Run Free</em> by Charles Payseur doesn’t really have a point and is stupid, to boot. The story is about rivers becoming corporeal entities, and they’re running from an empire that hunts and exploits them. In the opening scene, the river-people exploit their watery nature to defeat their enemies, then a pair of river-people have sex, and it’s just like humans. Okay, I don’t need a description, but the author gave it to us anyway. Why have it be that way if the rivers aren’t actually people? I get it, the rivers are about passion and wild emotion, and the author mentions something about water resources being exploited.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Look, I don’t disagree with the author, but I contend the story isn’t great. My problem is <em>Rivers Run Free</em> is far more about the author than anything else. Another minor complaint is that, again, the story isn’t really sci-fi or fantasy. It could vaguely be either so I don’t really count it as either.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>I will briefly give a shout-out to <em>ZeroS</em> by Peter Watts whose unintended message is ‘killing children is actually fine’ with the condition that they’re child soldiers.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Now, I want to talk about some of the stories I liked that weren’t sci-fi. The highlight was the third story, <em>You Will Always Have Family: a Triptych</em> by Kathleen Kayembe (which I think would’ve been a far better first story, even the second, a somewhat milquetoast Urban Fantasy story). It’s a heart-warming story about patricide. It’s basically a ghost story using Conglese and American folklore.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Another quite good one is <em>Carnival Nine</em> by Caroline M. Yoachim. It’s about small toys creating a civilization. It isn’t about much but the trials and tribulations of life. For me that’s too much of a vague message for me to care much, but it’s well written and emotional</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The last good one I want to mention is The Orange Tree by Maria Dahvana Headley is a story based off the Andalusian philosopher and Jewish Philosopher Solomon Ibn Gabirol as he animates a golem. It doesn’t have too much of a message, but it’s nice, and it feels like The Alchemist, a book I enjoyed.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The good fantasy story is <em>Tasting Notes on the Varietals of the Southern Coas</em>t by Gwendolyn Clare, about a vintner on the campaign trail of the emperor\'s army rating wine while horrible atrocities are going on all around.  The two good sci-fi stories are <em>The Greatest One-Star Restaurant</em> by Rachael K. Jones and<em> Zen and the Art of Starship</em> by Tobias S. Buckell. The former is about an automaton literally cannibalizing her fellow outlaw robots to make a restaurant, and the last one is about passive resistance. I read <em>Zen and Art of Motorcycle Maintenance</em> in high school, and I remember not liking it, so I expected worse. But it was powerful and a good read. It was also the last story in the book, which is a good thing. As I’ve said before, the first and last chapter of any book are its most important.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Speaking of beginnings, a lot of these stories have pretty rough ones, and that’s why I didn’t go story by story. It’s a bit more forgiving in books when you have the whole space to grow into the characters, but in a short story you have a relatively little time to get people interested. This book actually reminded me of why I don’t usually read short stories. I think the time is when they’re all by the same author. Stephen King is really dynamite at this though his literary quality isn’t what I desire anymore.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'Best American Science Fiction and Fantasy', '', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', 'best-scifi-and-fantasy-2018', '', '', '2021-03-13 06:37:37', '2021-03-13 06:37:37', '', 0, 'https://benyakiredits.com/?p=696', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(697, 2, '2020-08-24 16:26:51', '2020-08-24 16:26:51', '<!-- wp:paragraph {\"fontSize\":\"large\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-large-font-size\">Rating: 3.5 stars</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'Review of The Best American Science Fiction and Fantasy, 2018  - Edited by N.K. Jemisin', '', 'inherit', 'closed', 'closed', '', '696-revision-v1', '', '', '2020-08-24 16:26:51', '2020-08-24 16:26:51', '', 696, 'https://benyakiredits.com/uncategorized/696-revision-v1/', 0, 'revision', '', 0),
(718, 2, '2021-01-03 04:22:57', '2021-01-03 04:22:57', '<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>I haven\'t written anything on this site in awhile, and I\'ll address why that\'s happening. I\'ve been writing stuff, yes, but I\'ve also been learning programming. I\'ve learned a fair amount, and included within that is PHP! So, at some point, I\'ll be changing my blog to use my own WP theme - fancy! I\'ve also made my own PyPi package, a generic Flask site that uses it, and I\'m working on some other things. I\'ve been scrambling like heck to do this before my second child is born, so there hasn\'t been that much time for much of anything. It could be called a good use of time during the pandemic, but it\'s also incredibly tiring and, at times, depressing. I miss writing terribly. You can decide where that squinting modifier goes. At some point, I\'ll post links to all the things that I\'ve done, but that takes time, and if there\'s anything that\'s true of me, I\'m lazy. Or I\'m prioritizing other things, namely sleep/food/rearing my firstborn/learning the programming so I finally get what I need to do done.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":720,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"https://benyakiredits.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/Screen-Shot-2021-01-04-at-10.52.12-AM.png\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-image-720\"/></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->', 'The Silence: Or how it\'s going to keep going on for awhile. But not forever.', '', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', 'the-silence-or-how-its-going-to-keep-going-on-for-awhile-but-not-forever', '', '', '2021-03-13 04:40:31', '2021-03-13 04:40:31', '', 0, 'https://benyakiredits.com/?p=718', 0, 'post', '', 0),
